# TEMPLATE — operator action required.
#
# The dead man's switch ping URL. Alertmanager's 'deadman' receiver POSTs the
# always-firing Watchdog alert here every 5 minutes; healthchecks.io alerts YOU
# when those pings stop. See the Watchdog route in values.yaml for the why.
#
# This URL is a capability, not just an address — anyone holding it can keep the
# check green and mask a real outage. Hence SOPS, and hence url_file rather than
# an inline url in the (plaintext) values ConfigMap.
#
# ── 1. Create the check ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   https://healthchecks.io  →  sign up (free tier: 20 checks, ample for this)
#   Add Check:
#     Schedule: SIMPLE (not Cron) — the ping is a free-running interval, not
#               wall-clock aligned, and the two HA replicas never re-sync, so a
#               cron expression would flag normal drift as late.
#     Name    : melodic-muse cluster alerting
#     Period  : 6 minutes      # NOT 5 — see the measured-cadence note below
#     Grace   : 15 minutes     # 6m + 15m = 21m worst-case detection
#
#   MEASURED CADENCE IS 5m03s, NOT 5m00s. group_interval sets the nominal 5m,
#   but each ping slips by the POST round-trip, so real gaps are 5m03–5m04s
#   (measured 2026-08-16 over consecutive pings). A Period of 5m therefore puts
#   the check into amber "late" for ~3s of every cycle, permanently, and you
#   lose "late" as a signal. Period 6m absorbs the slip.
#
#   repeat_interval (1m) must stay strictly UNDER group_interval so every tick
#   passes the dedup check. Setting the two equal silently stretches the gap to
#   as much as ~10m. Alertmanager LOGS A WARNING for repeat < group
#   ("Notifications will not repeat until the next group_interval") — that
#   warning is expected and benign here.
#
#   `amtool check-config` does NOT catch repeat_interval < group_interval —
#   it returns SUCCESS. Only the running server warns. Do not treat a passing
#   check-config as validation of the timing.
#
#   Getting Period wrong is bad in both directions: too low parks the check
#   permanently in amber "late"; too high is worse and quieter — detection just
#   gets slower than you think it is, with nothing on screen to say so.
#
#   NOTIFICATIONS: send to gjcourt+critical@gmail.com — NOT +alerts, which is
#   the skip-inbox tier this repo documents as unread. Total alerting failure
#   outranks any individual critical, so it belongs on the paging address.
#   Configure at least one channel that does NOT depend on this cluster; also
#   prefer at least one NON-EMAIL channel (Pushover/ntfy/SMS), because a 21m
#   detection time becomes overnight detection if it only lands in a mailbox.
#   Copy the ping URL; it looks like https://hc-ping.com/<uuid>
#
# ── 2. Encrypt it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   NOTE: .sops.yaml is gitignored, so it exists only in the MAIN checkout —
#   a worktree has no copy and `sops -e` there would match no creation_rule.
#   Run this from ~/src/homelab (or copy .sops.yaml into the worktree first):
#
#     cp secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml.example secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml
#     # paste the ping URL into stringData.ping-url
#     sops -e -i secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml   # rule: infra/.*secret.*\.yaml$
#     git add secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml && git commit
#
#   Then VERIFY the encryption actually took — this catches the silent-plaintext
#   case whatever its cause:
#
#     grep -q '^sops:' secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml || echo "PLAINTEXT — STOP"
#     grep -c 'hc-ping.com' secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml   # must be 0
#
# secret-alertmanager-deadman.yaml is already referenced by kustomization.yaml,
# so `kustomize build` (and CI) fails until the encrypted file exists — that is
# the intended gate, and it also prevents a half-configured Alertmanager (which
# will not start with a missing mounted secret) from reaching the cluster.
# The infra-controllers Flux Kustomization has SOPS decryption enabled, so no
# further wiring is needed.
#
# ── 3. Verify after merge ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   3a. Fires at all. The check should flip to "up" within a minute or so of
#   the reconcile — group_wait is 0s, so the first flush waits only for cluster
#   gossip to settle (~seconds), not for a full period.
#
#   ONLY ONE REPLICA'S COUNTER MOVES. In HA the peer that wins the dedup race
#   is the one that POSTs, so alertmanager_notifications_total{integration=
#   "webhook"} stays 0 on the other. Check BOTH replicas before concluding the
#   webhook is dead — a single-replica read is how you talk yourself into
#   "fixing" a working deadman:
#
#     for p in 0 1; do kubectl -n monitoring exec \
#       alertmanager-kube-prometheus-stack-alertmanager-$p -c alertmanager -- \
#       wget -qO- http://localhost:9093/metrics \
#       | grep '^alertmanager_notifications_total{integration="webhook"}'; done
#
#   DO NOT SKIP THIS. Encrypting this file without replacing the placeholder
#   below clears every automated gate: kustomize build passes, Flux applies,
#   Alertmanager starts normally. url_file is read at send time, not validated
#   at config load, so the only symptom is failing POSTs. That does eventually
#   page via AlertmanagerClusterFailedToSendAlerts (critical), but the check
#   flipping to "up" is the direct confirmation.
#
#   3b. Fails CLOSED — the half that actually matters. Do NOT use the "pause"
#   button for this: pausing SUSPENDS monitoring, so a paused check never goes
#   down and never alerts. You would wait out the grace period, see nothing,
#   and learn nothing.
#
#   Instead stop the pings at the source with a temporary silence, then let the
#   check starve. amtool ships inside the container, so no port-forward needed:
#
#     kubectl -n monitoring exec alertmanager-kube-prometheus-stack-alertmanager-1 \
#       -c alertmanager -- amtool silence add 'alertname=Watchdog' \
#       --duration=25m --comment="deadman fail-closed test" \
#       --alertmanager.url=http://localhost:9093
#
#   The silence AUTO-EXPIRES, so an abandoned test self-heals rather than
#   leaving the deadman blind. Within Period + Grace (21m) healthchecks.io
#   emails that the check is down.
#
#   RECOVERY IS NOT INSTANT. After the silence lifts, the next flush waits for
#   a full group_interval — measured 4m37s. Do not read a few quiet minutes as
#   a failed recovery.
#
#   VERIFIED 2026-08-16 (results, so a future run has something to compare to):
#     last ping 09:11:11Z → DOWN at 09:32:11Z  = exactly 21m0s (Period+Grace)
#     silence expired 09:36:34Z → ping resumed 09:41:11Z = 4m37s recovery
#     downtime 8m59s; healthchecks.io "Total Pings" matched the Alertmanager
#     counter exactly at both points, i.e. zero ping loss cluster→endpoint.
#   An untested deadman is an assumption, not a safety net.
#
# ── 4. Rotation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   Owner: the healthchecks.io account on gjcourt@gmail.com. If that account
#   lapses or is closed, this secret is inert and the deadman is silently gone —
#   nothing in-cluster will notice, because a dead check cannot alert on itself.
#
#   To rotate (leak, account change, or routine): create the NEW check first,
#   re-encrypt this secret with the new URL, merge, and confirm the new check
#   goes green BEFORE deleting the old one. Overlapping the two avoids a
#   detection gap. A leaked URL is not merely readable — anyone holding it can
#   keep the check green and mask a real outage — so treat a leak as urgent
#   rather than cosmetic. Never paste the URL into a shell, a chat window, or
#   anything that keeps a transcript.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: alertmanager-deadman
  namespace: monitoring
type: Opaque
stringData:
  ping-url: "REPLACE_WITH_HEALTHCHECKS_IO_PING_URL"
